    'Evidence workflows and documentation that survive an audit',
    'Process refinement so compliance is a side effect of good operations',
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'What is the difference between SOC 2 Type I and Type II?',
      a: 'A SOC 2 Type I report evaluates whether your controls are designed properly at a point in time. A Type II report evaluates whether those controls operated effectively over a period of time (typically 3-12 months). Most clients ultimately want Type II; Type I is often the practical first step.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does my small business need SOC 2?',
      a: 'If you provide services to other businesses — especially SaaS, IT, or data-processing services — your clients may ask for a SOC 2 report. It is increasingly a condition of enterprise contracts. If you are not being asked, it may not be the right framework for you yet.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How long does a SOC 2 project take?',
      a: 'A Type I project typically takes 3-9 months depending on your starting point. Type II adds an observation period of 3-12 months on top of that. Much depends on how much evidence and documentation already exists.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What does a SOC 2 engagement cost?',
      a: 'Costs vary widely by scope and firm. Independent consultants can often deliver a large share of the readiness work for a fraction of what a Big 4 firm charges, and you keep a direct relationship with the person doing the work.',
    },
  ]}
/>
